  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/1da6182ac4999453b1c7e5f4c7db4d0a7e42f285463e8fe35aac5b8df46eb400.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-(x+2)(x-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=4$ and $a+b=2.$",
